eafe04df31994","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/hoeken/signalk-container-helper/commits/1bad3f67cc6cdb2242bb34a051deafe04df31994"}],"sha":"8073a217cc5f9428a8533c3f586336b3e615f99d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/hoeken/signalk-container-helper/commits/8073a217cc5f9428a8533c3f586336b3e615f99d"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/86628605?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/dirkwa","id":86628605,"login":"dirkwa","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjg2NjI4NjA1","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/hoeken/signalk-container-helper/commits/1bad3f67cc6cdb2242bb34a051deafe04df31994/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-23T20:47:55Z","email":"86628605+dirkwa@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Dirk Wahrheit"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-23T20:47:55Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat: resolve a managed endpoint without a ManagedContainer (#48)\n\n* feat: resolve a managed endpoint without a ManagedContainer\n\nresolveEndpoint''s managed path required a ManagedContainer, but used it for\nexactly one call: container.resolveAddress(port). That method needs a\nmanager, a name and a debug sink — no lifecycle state, no start() having\nrun. The coupling was incidental, and it locked out the majority of\nconsumers: of the three reference plugins only signalk-backup uses\nManagedContainer, while signalk-tailscale and signalk-questdb call\nensureRunning/pullImage/stop directly across dozens of call sites.\nConverting those to ManagedContainer to reach one method would be a rewrite\nof the container lifecycle, not a migration.\n\nSo accept { containerName, manager? } as an alternative to { container }.\nAdditive — the existing form is untouched, and endpoint.container is null in\nthe new form, so narrow on mode.\n\nThe resolution logic moves to src/resolve-address.ts and ManagedContainer now\ndelegates to it, so there is one implementation rather than two. That also\nhands the new form a fix the hand-rolled copies lack: matching by\nunprefixedName, falling back to any <namespace>-<name>. Both plugins that\ncopied this logic hardcoded `sk-${name}`, which silently finds nothing under\na non-default SIGNALK_CONTAINER_NAMESPACE — the same bug signalk-backup\nrecorded fixing when it adopted the helper.\n\n* docs: mode managed does not imply a container is present\n\n* refactor: name the export so it does not shadow the manager method\n\nSelf-audit. `resolveContainerAddress` was the ONLY export in this library\nthat collides with a ContainerManagerApi method name, and the two have\ndifferent signatures — the export takes (manager, name, port, debug), the\nmethod takes (name, port). TypeScript rejects a confused call, so this was\nnever a correctness risk, but it reads badly at a call site that has both in\nscope.\n\nThe siblings already avoid this: resolveMount wraps resolveHostPath and is\nnamed for what it returns, not for the manager call it makes.\nresolveContainerEndpoint follows that.\n\nAlso pins three cases the suite did not cover, each verified to fail against\na sloppier implementation: a manager missing either method (feature detection\nis normal here, and neither absence may throw), a binding whose port merely\nends with the requested digits (19000 must not satisfy 9000), and a udp\nbinding on the same port number.\n\n* test: pin why the resolver is tried before live port bindings\n\nA review proposed inverting this — check the live TCP binding first, fall\nback to the resolver — on the reading that the resolver is a cache that can\ngo stale. It is not. It is the authority on which of signalk-container''s\nthree address strategies applies, and the bindings are only meaningful in\none of them:\n\n- bare-metal: 127.0.0.1:<allocated host port>   (bindings agree)\n- container DNS: sk-<name>:<containerPort>      (no host port published)\n- shared netns: 127.0.0.1:<containerPort>       (a published binding is the\n  WRONG address — containers share the namespace)\n\nVerified against signalk-container''s own dispatch: the DNS branch sets\nnetworkMode and drops port bindings entirely, and the shared-netns branch\nreturns the internal port. Inverting would silently pick a published host\nport in both, so the fallback stays a fallback.\n\nRejected the finding and pinned the reasoning instead — the test fails\nagainst exactly the proposed inversion, which a comment alone would not have.","tree":{"sha":"920332918d5b6747fdf226211040f35d56c88751","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/hoeken/signalk-container-helper/git/trees/920332918d5b6747fdf226211040f35d56c88751"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/hoeken/signalk-container-helper/git/commits/1bad3f67cc6cdb2242bb34a051deafe04df31994","verification":{"payload":"tree 920332918d5b6747fdf226211040f35d56c88751\nparent f53156826c6aad814d46c7beaf99ee9a0251916b\nauthor Dirk Wahrheit <86628605+dirkwa@users.noreply.github.com> 1787518075 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1787518075 +1200\n\nfeat: resolve a managed endpoint without a ManagedContainer (#48)\n\n* feat: resolve a managed endpoint without a ManagedContainer\n\nresolveEndpoint''s managed path required a ManagedContainer, but used it for\nexactly one call: container.resolveAddress(port). That method needs a\nmanager, a name and a debug sink — no lifecycle state, no start() having\nrun. The coupling was incidental, and it locked out the majority of\nconsumers: of the three reference plugins only signalk-backup uses\nManagedContainer, while signalk-tailscale and signalk-questdb call\nensureRunning/pullImage/stop directly across dozens of call sites.\nConverting those to ManagedContainer to reach one method would be a rewrite\nof the container lifecycle, not a migration.\n\nSo accept { containerName, manager? } as an alternative to { container }.\nAdditive — the existing form is untouched, and endpoint.container is null in\nthe new form, so narrow on mode.\n\nThe resolution logic moves to src/resolve-address.ts and ManagedContainer now\ndelegates to it, so there is one implementation rather than two. That also\nhands the new form a fix the hand-rolled copies lack: matching by\nunprefixedName, falling back to any <namespace>-<name>. Both plugins that\ncopied this logic hardcoded `sk-${name}`, which silently finds nothing under\na non-default SIGNALK_CONTAINER_NAMESPACE — the same bug signalk-backup\nrecorded fixing when it adopted the helper.\n\n* docs: mode managed does not imply a container is present\n\n* refactor: name the export so it does not shadow the manager method\n\nSelf-audit. `resolveContainerAddress` was the ONLY export in this library\nthat collides with a ContainerManagerApi method name, and the two have\ndifferent signatures — the export takes (manager, name, port, debug), the\nmethod takes (name, port). TypeScript rejects a confused call, so this was\nnever a correctness risk, but it reads badly at a call site that has both in\nscope.\n\nThe siblings already avoid this: resolveMount wraps resolveHostPath and is\nnamed for what it returns, not for the manager call it makes.\nresolveContainerEndpoint follows that.\n\nAlso pins three cases the suite did not cover, each verified to fail against\na sloppier implementation: a manager missing either method (feature detection\nis normal here, and neither absence may throw), a binding whose port merely\nends with the requested digits (19000 must not satisfy 9000), and a udp\nbinding on the same port number.\n\n* test: pin why the resolver is tried before live port bindings\n\nA review proposed inverting this — check the live TCP binding first, fall\nback to the resolver — on the reading that the resolver is a cache that can\ngo stale. It is not. It is the authority on which of signalk-container''s\nthree address strategies applies, and the bindings are only meaningful in\none of them:\n\n- bare-metal: 127.0.0.1:<allocated host port>   (bindings agree)\n- container DNS: sk-<name>:<containerPort>      (no host port published)\n- shared netns: 127.0.0.1:<containerPort>       (a published binding is the\n  WRONG address — containers share the namespace)\n\nVerified against signalk-container''s own dispatch: the DNS branch sets\nnetworkMode and drops port bindings entirely, and the shared-netns branch\nreturns the internal port. Inverting would silently pick a published host\nport in both, so the fallback stays a fallback.\n\nRejected the finding and pinned the reasoning instead — the test fails\nagainst exactly the proposed inversion, which a comment alone would not have.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqi1x7CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAp/YQAJfCd2RLQM2o4BILoMc5C4AX\nvZbe6Na7YmhOxMl/oXcPzg2j8fryHwlpWpPZIivKyDlDeRm7bVwwLB6cpDGEaQYo\nCMMcBE7TFwBV4EvikjKcnyULNroSSiF5dexLutZ1vKPGH3UPI7Q4f13FYTKxjVU9\nohzuOq1/oGBFdUAcNDg1OmcruNuBJHQTzQ8TyRq5hKPo/hTO0IFrjbVHXWJxv6rd\ni90kDO6GzvlfCv0DScwcoUbCoISxY74uj11zAFAiiL5+F5rE5uWJUOFZM3Ij3iLV\nvwvEF8DZQ/TN6N/rRIvVU4tcCqemlXpDycZ10Q7tZYXwz+4VByqMOEpy76MQQKit\nejt0SIKB5qDA4hYktfYKChqJpVw5MS4bapMYDcLo4u9waHVZjJT4hpk1d8bxOjET\nfQ18LW8yf6CcKaw5Utin9Dld2HixRmqddGqut+dfnFi66n9MjpBv/S3nsfcfKL3P\nUASKICEac8K3syO+4AaAnskNBNKDT3D8ddw0kuJcJvRCxLH3HHmRlO38oUnReyg8\nWWqmGgbxUcfuB7j2Z3yht49wDbhZzU2o2RyM0r1HFtEL5+ypnt7p