eb-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/hoeken/signalk-container-helper/commit/7963f2726a08fef1444ecc60844ada2bccf64a11","node_id":"C_kwDOTgx649oAKDc5NjNmMjcyNmEwOGZlZjE0NDRlY2M2MDg0NGFkYTJiY2NmNjRhMTE","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/hoeken/signalk-container-helper/commit/c2c4e1408d379086917418c0121cf35377fe6ff9","sha":"c2c4e1408d379086917418c0121cf35377fe6ff9","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/hoeken/signalk-container-helper/commits/c2c4e1408d379086917418c0121cf35377fe6ff9"}],"sha":"7963f2726a08fef1444ecc60844ada2bccf64a11","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/hoeken/signalk-container-helper/commits/7963f2726a08fef1444ecc60844ada2bccf64a11"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/86628605?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/dirkwa","id":86628605,"login":"dirkwa","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjg2NjI4NjA1","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/hoeken/signalk-container-helper/commits/c2c4e1408d379086917418c0121cf35377fe6ff9/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-10T18:17:39Z","email":"86628605+dirkwa@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Dirk Wahrheit"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-10T18:17:39Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Cancellable and serialized lifecycle operations (#21)\n\n* feat: cancellable and serialized lifecycle operations\n\nEvery consumer plugin — signalk-backup, signalk-updater, signalk-doctor,\nmayara — carries its own abort machinery around these calls, because the\nlibrary offers neither cancellation nor mutual exclusion. signalk-questdb\nneeds ~85 lines of lifecycle mutex, generation counter and AbortController\nto use ManagedContainer safely. That belongs here.\n\nstart() and applyUpdate() take an optional OperationOptions carrying an\nAbortSignal. An aborted operation rejects with ContainerHelperError code\n\"cancelled\", flagged reported so startSafely logs it rather than surfacing\na plugin error for a stop the caller asked for.\n\nCancellation is cooperative, and the docs say so plainly. signalk-container\nexposes a signal only on its one-off job API — EnsureRunningOptions and\nstop() take none — so a call already in flight runs to completion. What the\nsignal cancels is everything around it: the manager-global wait, the drift\nprobe, readiness polling, and each step boundary. Those carry the\nminute-scale deadlines, and cancelling them is what stops an abandoned start\nfrom working against a container the caller has already torn down.\nwaitForContainerManager and waitForHttpReady take a signal for the same\nreason.\n\nstop() deliberately does NOT take one: an unregister plus one uninterruptible\nmanager.stop has nothing to cancel, and accepting a signal there would\npromise what it cannot deliver.\n\nOperations are also serialized per instance. An overlapping start and stop —\na plugin restarted while its first start is still waiting on readiness — now\nqueue instead of interleaving, so stop cannot land between ensureRunning and\nthe readiness poll and leave the plugin believing it started something it\njust removed. Both settlement paths advance the chain, so one rejection\ncannot wedge later calls. Callers holding their own lock see no change.\n\nVerified the five new tests fail against the unmodified implementation.\n\nCloses #20\n\n* fix: distinguish a cancelled readiness wait from a failed one\n\nTwo defects found auditing the branch.\n\nwaitForHttpReady threw its \"did not become ready within Nms\" error when\naborted mid-poll, because the abort exited through the same break as the\ndeadline. A caller that cancelled could not tell its own stop from a genuine\nreadiness failure, and awaitReadiness only avoided reporting one by the\naccident of calling throwIfAborted before this.fail. It now throws an\nAbortError DOMException, matching what fetch itself throws, and checks the\nsignal after the request as well as before.\n\nthrowIfAborted''s doc claimed signalk-container honours AbortSignal on\nensureRunning. It does not -- only the one-off job API takes one, which is\nthe whole reason cancellation here is cooperative. The line was a leftover\nfrom a wrong first assumption and contradicted what the rest of the PR is\ncareful to explain.\n\nAlso verified serialization cannot deadlock: a stop() queued behind a start()\nhung on an absent manager resolves in ~200ms via the signal rather than\nwaiting out the full manager timeout.\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: dirkwa <dirkwahrtheit@gmail.com>","tree":{"sha":"dfc6b029d3e090f4469e993963190f9e836b2973","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/hoeken/signalk-container-helper/git/trees/dfc6b029d3e090f4469e993963190f9e836b2973"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/hoeken/signalk-container-helper/git/commits/c2c4e1408d379086917418c0121cf35377fe6ff9","verification":{"payload":"tree dfc6b029d3e090f4469e993963190f9e836b2973\nparent a2f85419c557013ab4ecc042a9303bb436a2ff1d\nauthor Dirk Wahrheit <86628605+dirkwa@users.noreply.github.com> 1786385859 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786385859 +1200\n\nCancellable and serialized lifecycle operations (#21)\n\n* feat: cancellable and serialized lifecycle operations\n\nEvery consumer plugin — signalk-backup, signalk-updater, signalk-doctor,\nmayara — carries its own abort machinery around these calls, because the\nlibrary offers neither cancellation nor mutual exclusion. signalk-questdb\nneeds ~85 lines of lifecycle mutex, generation counter and AbortController\nto use ManagedContainer safely. That belongs here.\n\nstart() and applyUpdate() take an optional OperationOptions carrying an\nAbortSignal. An aborted operation rejects with ContainerHelperError code\n\"cancelled\", flagged reported so startSafely logs it rather than surfacing\na plugin error for a stop the caller asked for.\n\nCancellation is cooperative, and the docs say so plainly. signalk-container\nexposes a signal only on its one-off job API — EnsureRunningOptions and\nstop() take none — so a call already in flight runs to completion. What the\nsignal cancels is everything around it: the manager-global wait, the drift\nprobe, readiness polling, and each step boundary. Those carry the\nminute-scale deadlines, and cancelling them is what stops an abandoned start\nfrom working against a container the caller has already torn down.\nwaitForContainerManager and waitForHttpReady take a signal for the same\nreason.\n\nstop() deliberately does NOT take one: an unregister plus one uninterruptible\nmanager.stop has nothing to cancel, and accepting a signal there would\npromise what it cannot deliver.\n\nOperations are also serialized per instance. An overlapping start and stop —\na plugin restarted while its first start is still waiting on readiness — now\nqueue instead of interleaving, so stop cannot land between ensureRunning and\nthe readiness poll and leave the plugin believing it started something it\njust removed. Both settlement paths advance the chain, so one rejection\ncannot wedge later calls. Callers holding their own lock see no change.\n\nVerified the five new tests fail against the unmodified implementation.\n\nCloses #20\n\n* fix: distinguish a cancelled readiness wait from a failed one\n\nTwo defects found auditing the branch.\n\nwaitForHttpReady threw its \"did not become ready within Nms\" error when\naborted mid-poll, because the abort exited through the same break as the\ndeadline. A caller that cancelled could not tell its own stop from a genuine\nreadiness failure, and awaitReadiness only avoided reporting one by the\naccident of calling throwIfAborted before this.fail. It now throws an\nAbortError DOMException, matching what fetch itself throws, and checks the\nsignal after the request as well as before.\n\nthrowIfAborted''s doc claimed signalk-container honours AbortSignal on\nensureRunning. It does not -- only the one-off job API takes one, which is\nthe whole reason cancellation here is cooperative. The line was a leftover\nfrom a wrong first assumption and contradicted what the rest of the PR is\ncareful to explain.\n\nAlso verified serialization cannot deadlock: a stop() queued behind a start()\nhung on an absent manager resolves in ~200ms via the signal rather than\nwaiting out the full manager timeout.\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: dirkwa <dirkwahrtheit@gmail.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqehXDCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA6OsQAClV1DGqfYSY+J/H+fB+uPAX\nmZ8JBmmMAdgCFBBWEY+9L6e69IofygHEwe8oaR8Btcus/aOsD7iaHhd3WuI1F05H\neMTYU4YN7T4DCFQIl0EX4dqFYDQdOLul+Ze5jUioFVsNipDOCjRycArTJ8LcjtkQ\nHXihsWFj4eBWNSEENzCwPh92THQyhirgUCxt2FBT8Mu9t7Yto1RTj7LGSyriUGoR\n/4A77ln3dd5jM1CluW8qnMuQHW+ATkv0nWMmOgOq1BL8sZyJnvgolCr1rbIrxPaP\ndUKv1OGHYWqqLhK0XbfUdHif8XaxctW+7aZD4WEVNYXMU4INiinrheKKwUHVcE4W\nu2A1Q8SUgxsDObk7fPiYz0GleGrsjWMJTwOJjzgeVM3g96sByJS5x+9graNSWGsd\nEO3qPAEqS1GgtmxLZQLmh5ti7GLMwRjlln0RtR6OYze+SFz5IXPSzX0BWKvBtYXE\n5n5SxKPyaraO4SbLo3VJfswKZQG/kSadnmFeYRl0KbpoCdK2OXfJjOmu7wzxVGFy\nDuftj3pKhqZf4va2RgPW4gRzCbiKT+1cUPsz7F2mZ3F86qCSlNe4pgXf8kuE8soy\nBNz6rRG4YKc3/dBaBJHXAe1maO3mwLPgUvvT1w5yLzV0aqDk3Tfo344rPgDqknHG\n7Z41fhZGkxd0OgmHTAGr\n=cKuz\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-10T18:17:43Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"